 spaces volumes of this only  polynomial so in order to  represent faithfully the metric  information in a in a large  tree we would need a very high embedding  dimension in euclidean space but in  hyperbolic spaces only  a low dimensional  space and that fact we can  utilize in  in machine learning  so there's been a lot of recent work in  this area both on representation  learning aspects so understanding for a  given data set when a hyperbolic space  is useful so  understanding representation trade-offs  between different  geometric spaces and then once we have  settled on on the space that we want to  work with how can we efficiently  compute such representations  and then  after dealing with the representation  aspects also downstream machine learning  uh so now that we have a representation  in hyperbolic space um how can we extend  classical euclidean algorithms to these  settings  and that started with um empirical work  on extending classical algorithms like  support vector machines or craft neural  networks to hyperbolic spaces and then  more recently also work on  the theoretical analysis of these  algorithms and recovering um  complexity guarantees for instance  so to give one example of um  uh an algorithm where we have a  algorithm for both uh the euclidean case  and the hyperbolic case so if we look at  a very very basic problem of binary  classification and we assume our data is  separable and we want to learn a  separating hyperplane  to perform a classification then as you  all know in euclidean case we can use  the perceptron algorithm  if our data lies on a on a hyperbolic  manifold so in the  figure here um it's the lawrence model  one from parametrization of hyperbolic  space so we have data points on that  manifold  and then the equivalent problem would be  to learn a euclidean hyperplane here  shown in red  which  intersects our manifold and then the  intersection of the euclidean hyperplane  and the manifold is our precision  boundary  and that would give us a  and  hyperbolic analog of the perceptron  mechanism  and then we can come up with an  algorithm for training such a perceptron  algorithm and also  recover simple complexity guarantees for  instance and then starting from this  very basic algorithm we can extend this  to consider more complex tasks like  maybe learning a much much classifier or  really robust classifiers  nice effects from the geometric  alignment we can actually also observe  in instructors so there's  numerical results for for that as well  and just to mention a second  direction  optimization on metrics manifolds so as  i  initially showed some examples we often  find metrics valued subroutines in  machine learning so we have to optimize  over  a class of matrices under a certain  constraint so if we use euclidean  methods  we can perform a constrained  optimization task in in euclidean spaces  for instance constrained quickly  positive definiteness  and we can also view the problem we  formulate the problem on every money  manifold  in the case of positive definiteness we  can reformulate the problem on the  manifold of positive definite matrices  and then use free money and  unconstrained optimization  to solve an equivalent problem  and examples of such subroutines have  that have been studied in in the  euclidean and romanian setting  there are many many  examples  just a few are here on this slide so for  instance averaging gaussians  metrics completion uh synchronization of  data matrices those are all and problems  where romanian methods have been studied  a particularly  interesting feature of this switch to  the romanian setting is geodesic  convexity  so sometimes we have problems that are  in the euclidean setting  non-convex but that fulfill a weaker  notion of convexity on the respective  manifold so by switching the geometric  lens we gain  convexity and gain access to using tools  from convex optimization  on the on the manifold  my own work in this area has mostly  focused on constraint optimization  and so  where  we just look at this this very basic  formulation so we want to minimize the  function defined on every monument  manifold and we not only want to find  a minimum but we want to find the  minimum that that lies in a certain  compact uh geodesically convex subset of  the manifold  um and in prior work and remanual  optimization has mostly focused on an  unconstrained problem um constraint  problems  so so we were specifically focusing on  this additional difficulty of handling  constraints  and for that we looked into a romanian  version of the framework algorithm which  is a very popular algorithm for  constraint optimization in the euclidean  setting  in euclidean spaces that's been already  studied since the 1950s so very well  known  and established algorithm  in the interest of time i probably  can't say so much about the actual  extension of the algorithm but um let me  say that one can define a romanian  frankfurt algorithm that recovers  most of the the euclidean guarantees so  for for instance that guarantees a  sublinear convergence um to the to the  global optimum in the geodesically  convex case  um and just one note on this extension  of euclidean algorithms through romanian  algorithms it sometimes may seem as  um we're just switching out euclidean  tools for three million tools and then  most of the the proof ideas and  techniques goes through  unfortunately that's that's not always  the case so we often face um additional  difficulties and obstacles in the  romanian setting in this case here  the key difficulty lies in solving  the linear oracle which if you know the  euclidean frank wolfe it's like a  key subroutine in the algorithm and  that's in the euclidean setting a very  simple convex optimization problem but  in the remaining setting much much  harder to solve and requires additional  work to find efficient ways of solving  that  just a  non-geometry related side note i'm also  really interested in  control with with learning problems so  where we learn to  learn a control strategy in a setting  where we have very little time and very  little data so if anyone um also works  in that area i would like to chat about  that  [Music]  yeah yeah so  um for instance on positive definite uh  literacies and internal constraints  and you could also just numerically  solve an approximation and then if it's  like a reasonably good minimizer um you  can still recover something in your  convention  yeah so  there are um cases where this is  great  thank you

